almost two years since the start of the  pandemic the world is racing to contain  and understand a fast spreading and  potentially more dangerous variant of  the coronavirus on Friday a World Health  Organization panel named the variant  Omicron after it was first detected in  southern Africa it has already been  found in Belgium Israel and Hong Kong  the news prompted a wave of new  international travel restrictions  including here in the United States and  it rattled stock markets around the  world the Dow lost more than 900 points  or roughly 2 percent in a holiday  shortened session Friday and the S P 500  was down by more than 2 percent one  place was hit early in the outbreak is  South Africa Deborah patta begins our  coverage in Johannesburg  good morning Global attention is focused  here on South Africa Ground Zero for  this new variant before scientists have  been able to determine just how  dangerous this new Omicron strain is  countries have a rushed to impose tough  new travel restrictions the discovery of  the variant has sent a chill across a  pandemic weary World Omicron has over 30  mutations which scientists worry could  make it more transmissible and vaccine  resistant but amidst the heightened  anxiety there are voices of calm like  here at the Texas Children's Hospital  Center for vaccine development where Dr  Peter Hotes says there is no need to  panic it was troubling as all the  mutations in the spike protein are the  key is knowing how transmissible and  whether it has that ability to  out-compete Delta and and that bar is  high scientists are working around the  clock to determine just how bad the  variant is but a jittery world is not  waiting to find out and warnings by  global Health experts to avoid knee-jerk  responses have gone largely ignored the  U.S is joining countries in Europe and  Asia who have already closed their doors  to South Africa leaving International  Travelers frantic to get home it's  unfair it's unjustified  it's abrupt and it's not good for  tourism for South Africa South African  scientists say they are just 14 days  away from knowing how effective the  vaccines are against Omicron and both  moderna and Pfizer say if necessary they  can quickly adjust their vaccines  scientists tell us that Omicron was only  identified in South Africa because of  the country's excellent variant  surveillance and that in all likelihood  the new strain is already in many other  parts of the world for CBS Saturday  morning Deborah Potter Johannesburg
 

